<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lothian</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lothian is the region of south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/firth-of-forth">Firth of Forth</a> and the Lammermuir Hills, around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edinburgh">Edinburgh</a>. Home of the Votadini and the British kingdom of Gododdin, it was conquered by Northumbria in the 7th century and passed to the kings of Scots in the 10th and early 11th centuries, a gain secured after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-carham">Battle of Carham</a> around 1018.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lothian.jpg" alt="Lothian" width="320" height="482" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Brythones; derivative work by of Visitor from Wikishire · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lothian_County.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Lothian lies in the Scottish Lowlands, between the southern shore of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/firth-of-forth">Firth of Forth</a> and the Lammermuir Hills and Moorfoot Hills. Its principal settlement is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edinburgh">Edinburgh</a>, the Scottish capital, and other significant towns include Livingston, Linlithgow, Bathgate, Queensferry, Dalkeith, Musselburgh, Prestonpans, North Berwick, Dunbar and Haddington. Historically the name referred to a province covering most of what is now south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>.</p><p>The origin of the name is debated. It perhaps comes from the British Lugudūniānā, meaning country of the fort of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lugus">Lugus</a>, a Celtic god. Alternatively it may come from a stream now called the Lothian Burn. A popular legend derives it from King Lot of Arthurian legend. The usual <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> form is Laudonia.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In the post-Roman period Lothian was dominated by speakers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cumbric">Cumbric</a>, a British language closely related to Welsh, and Welsh tradition counts it part of the Old North. Angles settled it at an early stage, and it formed part of the kingdom of Bernicia, which extended south into present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northumberland">Northumberland</a> and Durham and later united with Deira as Northumbria. Place names show that English became firmly established here from the 6th century onwards. In the 7th century the region came under Bernicia's control, though the Angles' grip weakened after their defeat by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/picts">Picts</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-dun-nechtain">Battle of Nechtansmere</a>.</p><p>Roger of Wendover wrote that Edgar, King of the English, granted Lothian to Kenneth II, King of Scots, in 973, on condition that Kenneth attend the English court whenever the English king wore his crown. Medieval historians widely accept this as the point at which Lothian became part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>. Control was still not settled, and the Scots took the region at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-carham">Battle of Carham</a> in 1018, after which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/river-tweed">River Tweed</a> became the de facto Anglo-Scottish border. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-the-conqueror">William the Conqueror</a> invaded Lothian and crossed the Forth but could not conquer it.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The Northumbrian dialect of Old English, at first confined to Lothian and the Borders, spread across the Lowlands and became the Scots language. Lothian is one of the few areas of mainland <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> where Gaelic never dominated, though some Gaelic place names there are attributed to a Gaelic-speaking landowning aristocracy living there for 150 to 200 years.</p><p>By 1305 the area was divided into three shires, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edinburgh">Edinburgh</a>, Haddington and Linlithgow, informally called Midlothian, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-lothian">East Lothian</a> and West Lothian, hence the popular name the Lothians. They took those names in law in 1921, 1925 and 1947. A Lothian Regional Council, with four districts, governed the region from 1975 until 1996.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Lothian” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Malcolm II’s victory at the Battle of Carham, around 1018, secured <b>Lothian</b> between the Forth and the Tweed, and the British kingdom of Strathclyde was absorbed during the eleventh century.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-scotland">Kingdom of Scotland · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>They recognised West Saxon kings from Aethelstan onwards, while defending <b>Lothian</b> against Scottish attack.</blockquote><a href="/state/earldom-of-bamburgh">Earldom of Bamburgh</a></li><li><blockquote>Its centre was Din Eidyn, the rock of Edinburgh, and it stretched across <b>Lothian</b> and the eastern Borders.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-gododdin">Kingdom of Gododdin</a></li><li><blockquote>They descended from Iron Age communities of <b>Lothian</b> and the Borders, and their great hillfort at Traprain Law in East Lothian was a major centre from before the Roman arrival in the 80s.</blockquote><a href="/state/votadini">Votadini</a></li></ul></section>
